# Prolog Clause Style

When editing Prolog files, prefer idiomatic Prolog style:

- Prefer multiple clauses and head/body dispatch over `->` / `;` when the logic is simple ordered selection.
- Add `!` only when it preserves intended determinism, clause priority, or compatibility with existing behavior.
- Do not add `!` as a default optimization or style marker.
- If clause ordering alone is enough, prefer that over a cut.
- Keep `if-then-else` only when it is the clearest way to express the control flow or when replacing it would change semantics.

Example:

```prolog
% Good: clauses express the dispatch order; cuts preserve the original commit points.
type_encoder(OID, Value, Encoded) :-
    user_type(OID, _, Module),
    !,
    Module:encode(Value, Encoded).
type_encoder(OID, Value, Encoded) :-
    oid_type(OID, Type),
    !,
    encode_type(Type, Value, Encoded).
type_encoder(_, Value, Encoded) :-
    encode_default(Value, Encoded).
```
